      "description": "```\n============================================================================\nMemory disclosure and segfault in libtiff encoder via crafted TIFF tags\nLocation: src/encode.c:1014  in PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew()\nCommit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n============================================================================\n```\n\nA crafted TIFF file of a few hundred bytes makes Pillow copy an attacker-chosen amount of its own process memory into the TIFF it writes back out, or crash the process outright. The trigger is an ordinary `Image.open()` followed by a bare `im.save(fp, \"TIFF\")`. The only precondition is that the application re-saves images as TIFF. When the uploaded file is compressed, Pillow routes that save through libtiff automatically, with no options from the application. The attached reproducer does this with a 356-byte LZW TIFF: one crafted tag comes back holding 65536 bytes taken from `_imaging.so`'s read-only data, and a second crafted tag makes the worker die with SIGSEGV on every run.\n\nThe bug is in `PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew` (`src/encode.c`). It decides what arguments to pass to the variadic `TIFFSetField()` from the shape of the Python value (scalar, tuple or bytes). It assumes its `ImagingLibTiffMergeFieldInfo()` call defined how libtiff will read them. But `TIFFMergeFieldInfo()` never overrides libtiff's built-in field table, so libtiff reads the arguments according to its own definition of the tag:\n\n- For a counted array tag such as DNGPrivateData (50740), a count-1 integer in the source file is taken as the byte count. The data pointer is then read from an argument slot Pillow never wrote, and that many bytes are copied into the output.\n- For a fixed-size array tag such as ActiveArea (50829), the integer itself is used as the source address.\n\nWhere that unwritten slot points depends on the compiler. In the tested clang build it points into `_imaging.so`, and a larger count returned thousands of pointers into libpython, libc and libtiff, which defeats address-space randomisation for anyone chaining another bug. When the application preserves metadata with `tiffinfo=im.tag_v2`, a 64-bit entry turns ActiveArea into a 16-byte read from an address the attacker chooses. All observed memory errors are reads; no out-of-bounds write was found.\n\nOther branches of the same loop share the defect and are reachable from crafted TIFFs or from EXIF in JPEG/PNG/WebP re-saved as compressed TIFF. These are the `bytes`, RATIONAL-tuple and typed-tuple branches, and a BYTE-typed ColorMap. The file-reachable forwarding was introduced in `a3d45e9cefd0` (\"Added custom int and float TIFF tags\") and first released in 5.4.0.\n\n## Details\n\n`PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew` (`src/encode.c:777-1060`) chooses the argument\nlist it hands to the variadic `TIFFSetField()` from the *Python* value of each\ntag — scalar vs. tuple vs. `bytes`, and a type that is either autodetected or\ncopied from the source file's IFD — and assumes that its own\n`ImagingLibTiffMergeFieldInfo()` registration defines how libtiff will read the\n`va_list`. It does not: libtiff keeps its built-in definition for every tag it\nalready knows, and core tags are never registered at all. libtiff therefore\nconsumes the varargs as `(count, pointer)`, `(pointer)` or three pointers\naccording to *its* field table, whatever Pillow actually pushed. The encoder\nnever consults that table (`TIFFFindField` / `TIFFFieldPassCount` /\n`TIFFFieldWriteCount` / `TIFFFieldDataType`) before the call.\n\nThe most severe instance, and the one the attached reproducer exercises: when a\nTIFF is re-saved through the libtiff writer, every tag of the source IFD that\nPillow does not recognise is forwarded to libtiff with `TIFFSetField()`. A\ncount-1 integer entry is passed as a single scalar vararg. If libtiff's own\nfield table defines that tag number as an *array* field, libtiff consumes the\nvarargs as `(count, pointer)` or `(pointer)` instead, so the attacker's integer\nbecomes either the number of bytes to copy from a never-written vararg slot, or\nthe source address itself. The same mismatch is reachable through the typed\ntuple branches (tuple length consumed as a pointer for fixed-count built-in\ntags such as `CFARepeatPatternDim`, even when the source file is well-formed),\nthe `bytes`, `TIFF_RATIONAL`-tuple and core-tag branches of the same loop;\nthose are listed\nunder *Other branches with the same defect* below and need to be covered by the\nsame fix.\n\nA 181-byte TIFF (16×16 RGB, one LZW strip, plus one extra IFD entry\n`50740 / LONG / count 1 / value 4096` — DNGPrivateData) opened with\n`Image.open()` and saved with a bare `im.save(buf, \"TIFF\")` produces a 4296-byte\noutput whose tag 50740 holds 4096 bytes that occur nowhere in the input; scanning\n`/proc/self/mem` finds them in the read-only data segment of `_imaging.so`.\nChanging the extra entry to `50829 / LONG / 1 / 64` (ActiveArea) makes the same\ntwo calls segfault in `memcpy(dst, src=0x40, n=16)` under\n`ImagingLibTiffSetField` ← `PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew`. An ASan build of the\nextensions reports `READ of size 4096` in `memcpy` called from\n`src/encode.c:1014` for the first input.\n\n### Code path\n\n`_save` copies all tags of the opened image into the libtiff attribute list.\nFor a tag that is neither in `LIBTIFF_CORE` nor in `TiffTags.TAGS_V2`, no type\nis recorded, so the value goes through untyped:\n\n```python\n# src/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py:1782\nsupplied_tags = {**legacy_ifd, **getattr(im, \"tag_v2\", {})}\n…\n# :1959-1982\nfor tag, value in itertools.chain(ifd.items(), supplied_tags.items()):\n    if tag not in TiffTags.LIBTIFF_CORE:\n        …\n        elif isinstance(value, (int, float, str, bytes)) or …:\n            type = TiffTags.lookup(tag).type      # None for 50740, 50829, …\n            if type:\n                types[tag] = type\n    if tag not in atts and tag not in blocklist:\n        …\n            atts[tag] = value                     # plain int 4096\n```\n\n`compression` defaults to the source image's `im.info[\"compression\"]`\n(`TiffImagePlugin.py:1731-1746`), so a compressed source selects the libtiff\nwriter without the application asking for it.\n\nIn the encoder the untyped `int` is autodetected as `TIFF_LONG`\n(`src/encode.c:805-815`), the tag is \"registered\" as a scalar custom field, and\nthen set with one 32-bit vararg:\n\n```c\n// src/encode.c:844      (is_var_length = 0 → writecount 1, passcount 0)\nif (ImagingLibTiffMergeFieldInfo(&encoder->state, type, key_int, is_var_length)) {\n    … continue;\n}\n…\n// src/encode.c:1013-1016\n} else if (type == TIFF_LONG || type == TIFF_IFD) {\n    status = ImagingLibTiffSetField(\n        &encoder->state, (ttag_t)key_int, (UINT32)PyLong_AsLong(value)\n    );\n```\n\nThe registration does not do what the caller assumes. `TIFFMergeFieldInfo()`\nonly adds definitions for tags libtiff does not already know and returns 0\n(success) either way, so for tags in libtiff's built-in table the built-in\ndefinition stays in force. Queried against the linked libtiff 4.5.0 after\nexactly Pillow's merge call:\n\n```\n50740 DNGPrivateData      type=BYTE   passcount=1 writecount=-1   (unchanged)\n50829 ActiveArea          type=LONG   passcount=0 writecount=4    (unchanged)\n65000 CustomField         type=LONG   passcount=0 writecount=1    (Pillow's definition, tag was unknown)\n```\n\nlibtiff's setter for `FIELD_CUSTOM` tags then interprets the `va_list`\naccording to *its* definition: for `passcount=1` it reads `va_arg(ap, int)` as\nthe element count — the attacker's 4096 — allocates `count * size` bytes and\n`memcpy`s that many bytes from `va_arg(ap, void *)`, a slot\n`PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew` never wrote. On x86-64 that slot is register\n`rcx`; in this build it holds a pointer into `_imaging.so`'s `.rodata`, so the\ncopy succeeds deterministically (a value of 2 000 000 also succeeds and yields a\n2 000 200-byte output). `TIFFWriteDirectory` writes the copied bytes into the\noutput file as the tag's value. For fixed-count array fields (`passcount=0`,\n`writecount=4` for ActiveArea) there is no count argument, so the attacker's\nscalar is consumed as the `void *` source and 16 bytes are read from address\n64 → SIGSEGV.\n\nTags in libtiff 4.5's built-in table that Pillow forwards untyped and that take\nthe `(count, pointer)` form include DNGPrivateData 50740, CFAPattern 33422,\nCFAPlaneColor 50710, LinearizationTable 50712, BlackLevel 50714, WhiteLevel\n50717, ColorMatrix1/2 50721/50722, AsShotNeutral 50728, OriginalRawFileName/Data\n50827/50828, AsShotICCProfile 50831, CurrentICCProfile 50833. Fixed-count ones\n(pointer only → crash) include ActiveArea 50829, DNGVersion/DNGBackwardVersion\n50706/50707, DefaultScale/DefaultCropOrigin/DefaultCropSize 50718–50720,\nAsShotWhiteXY 50729, CFARepeatPatternDim 33421, BlackLevelRepeatDim 50713,\nImageLayer 34732, RawDataUniqueID 50781.\n\nWhen the application saves with `tiffinfo=im.tag_v2` (the documented way to\npreserve metadata), the file-declared type is honoured, and a `LONG8` entry\nreaches the `(uint64_t)PyLong_AsLongLong(value)` branch at\n`src/encode.c:1049-1052`. For ActiveArea that is a 16-byte read from a full\n64-bit attacker-chosen address into the output: a source entry\n`50829 / LONG8 / 1 / <address of a Python bytes object>` produced an output\nActiveArea of `(b'SECR', b'ET-T', b'OKEN', b'-012')` in testing.\n\nThe comment above `blocklist +=` at `TiffImagePlugin.py:1938-1944` already notes\nthat array tags with the wrong length \"will result in segfaults\"; the blocklist\ncovers only a handful of tags Pillow itself knows, not the ones libtiff knows and\nPillow does not. Forwarding of these tags was introduced in `a3d45e9cefd0`\n(\"Added custom int and float TIFF tags\", 2018-10-25), first released in 5.4.0;\nreleases 2.0.0–3.0.0 forwarded them as scalars too, until `9fa6634f4`\n(\"Partial fix for #1597\") limited `_save` to `LIBTIFF_CORE`.\n\n### Other branches with the same defect\n\nThe scalar-integer branch above is one of several places in the same loop\nwhere the call shape is derived from the Python value rather than from\nlibtiff's definition. All of the following were confirmed on this commit (stock\nbuild crashes and/or ASan reports, stack always `memcpy ← libtiff ←\nImagingLibTiffSetField src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:934 ←\nPyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew`), and all are reachable from a sub-1 KB crafted\nfile followed by an ordinary `save()`:\n\n* **Typed tuple → `(len, av)` for a fixed-count built-in field**\n  (`src/encode.c:904-994`, the SHORT/LONG/SBYTE/SSHORT/SLONG/FLOAT/DOUBLE\n  tuple branches all call `ImagingLibTiffSetField(..., len, av)`). For libtiff\n  built-in fields with `passcount=0` and a fixed `writecount` —\n  `CFARepeatPatternDim` 33421 (SHORT×2), `BlackLevelRepeatDim` 50713\n  (SHORT×2), `ImageLayer` 34732 (LONG×2), `ActiveArea` 50829 (LONG×4),\n  `MatrixWorldToScreen/Camera` 33305/33306 (FLOAT×16) — libtiff takes the\n  *first* vararg as the source pointer, so the tuple length becomes the\n  `memcpy` source address. A perfectly well-formed 206-byte 8×8 PackBits TIFF\n  carrying `33421 / SHORT / 2 / (2, 2)` re-saved with a bare\n  `im.save(out, \"TIFF\")` dies with SIGSEGV in `memcpy` (gdb: `rsi = 0x2`, the\n  tuple length) on every run; so does\n  `Image.new(\"L\", (8, 8)).save(buf, \"TIFF\", compression=\"packbits\",\n  tiffinfo={33421: (2, 2)})`, and a JPEG whose EXIF carries the tag saved with\n  `exif=im.getexif()` into a compressed TIFF. `bytes` values for built-in\n  fixed-count BYTE tags (e.g. `DNGVersion` 50706, BYTE×4) crash the same way\n  through the `(PyBytes_Size, PyBytes_AsString)` call at `src/encode.c:860-865`.\n  The mirror image for `passcount=1` fields: `Photoshop` 34377 declared\n  SHORT, count 1, value 60000 takes the scalar branch at `:1009-1012`, libtiff\n  reads 60000 as the count and an unpassed vararg as the pointer, and the\n  re-save *succeeds* with a 60162-byte output whose tag 34377 holds 60000 bytes\n  of process memory (here C prototype strings from `_imaging.so`'s `.rodata`).\n* **`bytes` value → single `char *`** (`src/encode.c:805-815` autodetects\n  `bytes` as `TIFF_ASCII`; `:1033-1042` passes `PyBytes_AsString(value)` alone).\n  `ImageFileDirectory_v2._setitem` stores a BYTE-typed entry as bare `bytes`\n  and an ASCII-typed one as `str` (encoded to `bytes` by `_save`), so the\n  file's declared type selects this branch.\n  - `ColorMap` (320) declared BYTE with 4 bytes of data in a non-palette\n    PackBits TIFF: 320 is in `core_tags[]`, no type is consulted, the value\n    skips the 768-tuple `TIFFTAG_COLORMAP` branch (`:871-895`) and libtiff reads\n    three `uint16_t *` and copies `1 << BitsPerSample` shorts from each — a\n    512-byte over-read of the 5-byte Python bytes buffer, then dereference of\n    two stale argument registers → SIGSEGV on every run of\n    `Image.open(f); im.save(out, \"TIFF\")`. `COLORMAP` is not in `_save`'s\n    `blocklist`.\n  - `Photoshop` (34377) declared ASCII: `_save` copies value *and* type\n    (`TiffImagePlugin.py:1802-1818`; only `IPTC_NAA_CHUNK` has a BYTE/UNDEFINED\n    type guard there, from commit 2245df0a — `PHOTOSHOP_CHUNK` and `XMP` do\n    not). libtiff's definition is BYTE, `passcount=1`, so the low 32 bits of\n    the `char *` become the byte count for a `memcpy` from a stale register\n    (ASan: `READ of size 3518384`; stock: SIGSEGV in about half of runs,\n    otherwise `RuntimeError`). `ICCProfile` (34675) declared ASCII behaves the\n    same way via `im.info[\"icc_profile\"]` → `ifd[ICCPROFILE]`\n    (`TiffImagePlugin.py:1822-1824`).\n* **`TIFF_RATIONAL` tuple → array with no count** (`src/encode.c:995-1006`;\n  compare the `TIFF_FLOAT` branch at `:969-981`, which passes `len, av`).\n  - For a private tag (e.g. 50000) Pillow itself registers the field with\n    `passcount=1` (`TiffDecode.c:904-908`), so libtiff takes the low 32 bits of\n    `av` as the element count and the next stale vararg as the source:\n    `heap-buffer-overflow READ of size 119872 … right of 8-byte region` under\n    ASan (`119872 == 4 * low32(av)`), SIGSEGV in ~4 of 5 stock runs. Reached by\n    a JPEG whose EXIF carries one private RATIONAL×2 tag saved with\n    `im.save(out, \"TIFF\", compression=\"tiff_lzw\", exif=im.info[\"exif\"])`, or a\n    TIFF re-saved with `tiffinfo=im.tag_v2` (`_setitem` auto-types a tuple of\n    `IFDRational` as RATIONAL, `TiffImagePlugin.py:689-701`; `types[tag] =\n    ifd.tagtype[tag]` at `:1968`).\n  - For libtiff's built-in fixed-count float tags `WhitePoint` (318, 2),\n    `PrimaryChromaticities` (319, 6) and `YCbCrCoefficients` (529, 3), which\n    `_save` forwards from any source TIFF and types RATIONAL via\n    `TiffTags.lookup`, the pointer-only convention happens to match, but\n    libtiff copies its *fixed* count from an array that is only as long as the\n    file declared: a PackBits TIFF with `PrimaryChromaticities` count 1 and a\n    bare `im.save(out, \"TIFF\")` gives `READ of size 24 … right of 4-byte\n    region`, and the over-read floats are written to tag 319 of the output.\n    `REFERENCEBLACKWHITE`/`TRANSFERFUNCTION` are blocklisted at\n    `TiffImagePlugin.py:1941-1952` for exactly this reason; 318/319/529 are not.\n* **Core tag reached as a scalar via a stale file-supplied type.** With the\n  re-save idiom `im.save(out, \"TIFF\", tiffinfo=im.tag_v2)`, `_save` copies\n  `ifd.tagtype[key] = info.tagtype[key]` for every key\n  (`TiffImagePlugin.py:1768-1776`) and later assigns its own values to some of\n  the same tags without resetting the type (`ifd[COLORMAP] = colormap` at\n  `:1870-1877`; likewise BitsPerSample, SamplesPerPixel, ExtraSamples,\n  ICCProfile, …). For a 418-byte PackBits *palette* TIFF whose ColorMap entry\n  is declared BYTE, `_setitem` (`:751-770`) truncates `_save`'s 768-entry\n  palette to a single `int` (with only a \"too many entries\" warning), the\n  encoder receives `(320, 0)`, autodetects `TIFF_LONG`, and\n  `src/encode.c:1013-1016` calls `TIFFSetField(TIFFTAG_COLORMAP, (UINT32)0)` —\n  libtiff reads three `uint16_t *` and faults at an address ≤ 65280 on every\n  run. The stale-tagtype copy is a Python-side hygiene bug in its own right,\n  but its memory-safety consequence exists only because the encoder lets a\n  non-tuple value reach `TIFFSetField(COLORMAP)`.\n\n## Impact\n\nAny service that accepts a TIFF and writes it back out as TIFF through libtiff —\nwhich happens with a plain `im.save(fp, \"TIFF\")` whenever the uploaded file is\ncompressed, or whenever a `compression=` other than raw is requested — returns\nto the uploader, inside one metadata tag of the produced file, an\nattacker-chosen number of bytes read from a stale pointer in the worker process,\ndeterministically and without retries. Which memory that is depends on the\ncompiler's register allocation for `PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew`: in this clang\nbuild the pointer is `_imaging.so + 0x1588`, so a request for 262 144 bytes\n(which succeeded every time in testing, as did 2 000 000) covers the\nextension's `.rodata`, `.data.rel.ro`/GOT and `.data` and returned 2 800+\nresolved pointers into libpython, libc, libtiff, libjpeg and others — a complete\nASLR bypass for chaining with any other memory bug; a gcc build of the same\nsource left a different, unrelated address in that slot. The fixed-count\nvariant kills the worker process with SIGSEGV from a 181-byte upload on every\nrequest. With `tiffinfo=im.tag_v2` the source\naddress is fully attacker-chosen (16-byte arbitrary read into the output for\nActiveArea, or a crash). No non-default Pillow setting is involved; the only\nprecondition is TIFF-to-TIFF re-encoding.\n\nThe other branches add further deterministic process-kill inputs (BYTE-typed\nColorMap in any RGB or palette TIFF; `thumbnail()` keeps `tag_v2`, so\nopen→thumbnail→save-as-TIFF is affected too) and extend the reach to\nJPEG/PNG/WebP uploads whose EXIF is preserved with `exif=` when converting to\nlibtiff-compressed TIFF. All the memory errors are reads; libtiff sizes its\ndestination buffers with the same count it copies, so no out-of-bounds write\nwas found.\n\n## Reproducing\n\n`repro` is one Python script, run against the stock build with the installed `PIL`. It needs no compiler and no extra files.\n\n`upload(tag, value)` has Pillow write a 16×16 red RGB image as an LZW-compressed TIFF, then appends a new IFD holding the same entries plus one extra `<tag> / LONG / count 1 / value` (kept in tag order) and points the header at it — a 356-byte input on this tree. The source is LZW-compressed on purpose. A bare `im.save(out, \"TIFF\")` keeps the source's compression, so the save goes through the libtiff writer even though the application passes no arguments. `resave()` is the whole \"application\": `Image.open(BytesIO(src)); im.save(out, \"TIFF\")`.\n\nThe script runs three cases:\n\n1. **Control, tag 65000 = 4096.** libtiff does not know this tag, so the file is re-saved correctly and the output tag reads back as the integer `4096`. This is what should happen to every tag.\n2. **Leak, DNGPrivateData 50740 = 65536.** libtiff's built-in definition of this tag is a counted BYTE array. It reads Pillow's single scalar as the *count* and takes the data pointer from a vararg slot Pillow never wrote. The output tag comes back as `bytes` of length 65536 that do not occur in the input. The script prints the first 32 of them and the file offset at which they occur in `_imaging.so` on disk. On this build they always do (offset `0x57588`): the stale pointer aims into the extension's read-only data.\n3. **Wild read, ActiveArea 50829 = 64.** libtiff defines this tag as a fixed LONG[4] array with no count, so it uses the scalar 64 as the *source address*. The script re-runs itself as a child process (`repro child`, core dumps disabled) to do this re-save, and the child exits with `-11`, meaning SIGSEGV.\n\nExpected output on the affected tree:\n\n```\ncontrol  tag 65000=4096 -> 4096\nleak     tag 50740=65536 from a 356-byte upload -> 65536 bytes not present in input, head a661fcffc661fcff3360fcff7b61fcffd361fcff085ffcffe061fcff3360fcff\n         leaked bytes found in _imaging.so at file offset 0x57588\ncrash    tag 50829=64 -> child exit -11 (SIGSEGV)\nBUG\n```\n\nThe script prints `BUG` if case 2 leaks or case 3 crashes. Both are deterministic on this build. A fixed Pillow prints `OK`: the tag does not come back as 65536 bytes of foreign data (it reads back as `None`, or `save()` raises `ValueError`/`RuntimeError`, which the script catches), and the child exits with something other than SIGSEGV (0, or 1 if `save()` raised).\n\nThe other branches can be checked by hand on the stock build in the same way\n(each as `Image.open(f); im.save(BytesIO(), \"TIFF\")` in a child process):\na PackBits RGB TIFF with an extra IFD entry `320 / BYTE / 4 / \"ABCD\"` →\nSIGSEGV every run; a PackBits palette TIFF whose ColorMap is `320 / BYTE / 2`\nsaved with `tiffinfo=im.tag_v2` → SIGSEGV every run; a Pillow-written JPEG\nwhose EXIF holds tag 50000 = RATIONAL×2 saved with\n`compression=\"tiff_lzw\", exif=im.info[\"exif\"]` → SIGSEGV in most runs,\n`RuntimeError` (\"Failed to allocate memory for custom tag binary object\n(-N elements of 4 bytes each)\") in the rest.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nThe encoder must not guess the calling convention; it has to ask libtiff what\nthe tag's effective definition is *after* `ImagingLibTiffMergeFieldInfo` and\nmarshal accordingly, or refuse. The attached patch does this for non-core tags:\n\n* a new `ImagingLibTiffFindField()` in `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c` (declared\n  in `TiffDecode.h`, next to the other libtiff wrappers) returns libtiff's\n  `TIFFFindField` type, `passcount` and `writecount` for a tag;\n* in `PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew` (`src/encode.c`), for a non-core tag that\n  libtiff already knows, Pillow adopts libtiff's type when it has none of its\n  own, and after `ImagingLibTiffMergeFieldInfo` a new `tiff_field_matches()`\n  checks the value against libtiff's definition: a scalar only for a\n  single-value field whose C argument type (int / `uint64_t` / double /\n  `char *`) matches, an array only with libtiff's element size and, for\n  fixed-count fields, exactly `writecount` elements. Anything else raises\n  `ValueError(\"Tag value does not match libtiff field definition\")` instead of\n  reaching `TIFFSetField` — this covers the reproducer's DNGPrivateData and\n  ActiveArea inputs, ASCII-typed Photoshop, short PrimaryChromaticities and the\n  `LONG8` `tiffinfo=` variant;\n* every tuple/`bytes` branch now goes through `set_tiff_array_field()`, which\n  passes `len` only when the field has `passcount` set; this also fixes the\n  `TIFF_RATIONAL` branch, which never passed a count (and extends it to\n  `TIFF_SRATIONAL`);\n* `TIFF_UNDEFINED` fields are registered as variable-length, matching how their\n  `bytes` values are passed;\n* `Tests/test_file_libtiff.py` gains a regression test (scalar DNGPrivateData,\n  scalar and 2-tuple ActiveArea rejected; well-formed values round-trip).\n\nCore tags and tags libtiff does not know keep their current behaviour. With the\npatch applied to a clean checkout and rebuilt, the reproducer no longer\ntriggers and the test suite passes. It is not a complete fix, however: the\nwhole check sits inside `if (!is_core_tag)`, so the BYTE-typed ColorMap variant\nstays open. The PackBits RGB TIFF carrying `320 / BYTE / 4`, re-saved with a\nbare `save()`, no longer segfaults at this point but writes 1536 bytes —\nover-read from the 5-byte `bytes` object and two stale varargs, including 22\nlive heap pointers — into the output ColorMap, which is still a disclosure.\nBefore merging, it needs a guard that accepts `TIFFTAG_COLORMAP` only through\nthe existing 768-tuple branch, never through the scalar or `bytes` branches,\nand ideally the same shape check for the other core tags libtiff special-cases\nin `_TIFFVSetField` (YCbCrSubsampling 530, TransferFunction 301,\nReferenceBlackWhite 532).\n\nNarrower Python-side mitigations in `_save` close individual entry points but\nnot the class, and are worth adding alongside the C-side check: drop tags\nwith `TiffTags.lookup(tag).type is None` from `supplied_tags` (closes the `Image.open()→save()` path shown here but not\n`tiffinfo=` callers); remove `COLORMAP` from `supplied_tags` next to\n`EXIFIFD`/`SAMPLEFORMAT` and extend the `IPTC_NAA_CHUNK` BYTE/UNDEFINED type\nguard to `PHOTOSHOP_CHUNK` and `XMP`; add 318/319/529 to the blocklist next to\n`REFERENCEBLACKWHITE`; and have `_save` `ifd.tagtype.pop(tag, None)` before\nassigning its own ColorMap/BitsPerSample/ICCProfile/… values so a\n`tiffinfo=`-imported type cannot reshape them. The C-side check is the fix to\nrely on.\n\n## The patch\n\nPass TIFF tag values to libtiff according to its own field definition, and raise ValueError when they do not fit\n\nApplies to `src/encode.c` at the commit above. It passed the mechanical check — applied to a clean tree, rebuilt, the reproducer stopped firing — but a reviewer did not endorse it as the right fix.\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py b/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\nindex 3d804ea4a..adc4633e4 100644\n--- a/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\n+++ b/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\n@@ -395,6 +395,43 @@ class TestFileLibTiff(LibTiffTestCase):\n         with pytest.raises(ValueError, match=\"Incorrect tag value type\"):\n             im.save(out, tiffinfo=ifd)\n \n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+        \"tag, value\",\n+        (\n+            # DNGPrivateData is a BYTE array with a count to libtiff\n+            (50740, 4096),\n+            # ActiveArea is a LONG array of four values to libtiff\n+            (50829, 64),\n+            (50829, (0, 0)),\n+        ),\n+    )\n+    def test_libtiff_field_definition(\n+        self, tag: int, value: Any, monkeypatch: pytest.MonkeyPat